From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 00:35:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: seal a database's pages in application-services, for
 encriox

sql-support gains obfsvfs, for an app that keeps its databases sealed:
encriox, whose history, bookmarks, logins and cards are sealed with
keys from the user's vault (encriox W21). It is the scheme of
Gecko's obfuscating VFS, brought to application-services' own SQLite
with AES-256-GCM from NSS: each 8192-byte page, its log's and its
journal's, sealed under a key handed to obfsvfs by a handle, never in
the database's name, which rusqlite puts into the message of an open
that fails.

Each page carries, in its last 4 reserved bytes, a check of its
database's key: an HMAC-SHA-256 of a label of its own under the key,
which tells nothing of the key, and tells what a tag cannot: why a
page does not open. A key not the database's is refused at the open,
SQLITE_AUTH, before anything of it is opened or made. Under its own
key a page that does not open is an I/O error, SQLITE_IOERR_DATA,
never a corrupt database, which places and autofill delete and make
again; and a frame of the log that does not open is one a crash tore,
which ends the log there, as SQLite in the clear does.

On Android, where application-services has a SQLite of its own, it is
SQLite's default VFS before any database opens, and refuses a sealed
database opened without its key, and one in the clear with a key.

The stage 3 spike's patches 0001, 0005 and 0008, as one (encriox,
W21, W24).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
